 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(\text{PER\_FRAME} \cdot \text{RayMarchCost})$ per frame. The lattice update is $O(\text{LatticePoints})$ but occurs every `LIT_EVERY` frames.
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(\text{DIRS})$ for storing directions and radiance.
-- **Performance**: Constant per-frame cost due to `PER_FRAME` limiting active samples. The remaining `DIRS - PER_FRAME` samples retain last frame's answer [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Low cost, no spatial variation machinery, stable lighting.
-- **Limitations**: Single probe per room, no spatial variation, potential directional bias if not handled.
+The method maintains a constant per-frame cost by limiting active samples to `PER_FRAME`, with the remaining samples retaining the previous frame's answer [S1, unofficial]. The lattice update occurs every `LIT_EVERY` frames [S1, unofficial]. Strengths include low cost and stable lighting without spatial variation machinery [S1, unofficial]. Limitations include the single-probe constraint and potential directional bias if not handled [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the Wildforge voxel game engine, implemented in Rust. It is an unofficial technique described in the project's source code and documentation [S1, unofficial].
